  "publication_day": "2026-07-02",
  "article_opening": "Investigators allege that four individuals deliberately concealed the actual end-user of servers thought to contain advanced Nvidia AI chips.\nBy Sebastian Strangio\nSingaporean authorities said yesterday that they have seized a luxury bungalow and charged four people with money laundering as part of an investigation linked to the alleged movement of Nvidia chips subject to U.S. export controls.\nIn a statement , the Singapore Police Force (SPF) said that it had issued a “prohibition of disposal or",
  "passage": "…including Malaysia and Singapore .\nSingapore said that it is unaware of any smuggling taking place in the country, but that it would come down hard on any individuals or entities found to be breaching the law – a stance that the SPF reiterated in yesterday’s statement.\n“The police hold a zero-tolerance stance towards such offenses and will act resolutely against those, whether businesses or individuals, who violate our laws, and safeguard Singapore’s integrity as a trusted global financial and business hub underpinned by the rule of law,” the statement read.\nMalaysia has also promised to close any loopholes against the movement of advanced semiconductors through its jurisdiction. Last week, Malaysian authorities said they had foiled an attempt to smuggle advanced AI chips through the country’s main airport, seizing 72 servers worth nearly $13 million.\nThe U.S. government is probing whether the Hangzhou-based AI firm has built its disruptive R1 AI model on restricted Nvidia chips.\nThe U.S. government has accused the conglomerate and its chairman, Chen Zhi, of running a network of forced-labor scam centers.\nThe number of such cases more than tripled from 589 in the first half of 2024 to 1,762 over the same period this year, according to the Singaporean police.\nBy Marcus Loh\nHistory suggests that the city-state will maintain its steady, pragmatic course through increasingly turbulent international waters.\nBy Saqlain Rizve\nBy Chen Wangwanyu\nBy Toh Oon Hoe\nBy Catherine Putz\nBy Larry Luckey\nBy Laure-Anne Courdesse and Arntraud Hartmann\nBy Cat…",
  "statement": "Malaysian authorities said they had foiled an attempt to smuggle advanced AI chips through the country’s main airport, seizing 72 servers worth nearly $13 million"
 },
 {
  "id": "G503",
  "article_id": "gnews:dc1cc0cd2e4a2715a57ae235",
  "title": "Greg Lui Arrested Over Alleged $300 Million Nvidia Server Smuggling to China",
  "publication_day": "2026-10-03",
  "article_opening": "Greg Lui Arrested Over Alleged $300 Million Nvidia Server Smuggling to China\nGreg Lui of Earthmade is accused of shipping over $300 million in Nvidia servers to China through Malaysia and Singapore, federal prosecutors say.\nGreg Lui, 38, owner of California-based Earthmade Computer, was arrested on October 1, 2026 on charges of sending servers with Nvidia chips worth more than $300 million to China, sidestepping U.S. export licenses through two stopovers in Malaysia and Singapore. The Department",
  "passage": "…ervers with Nvidia chips worth more than $300 million to China, sidestepping U.S. export licenses through two stopovers in Malaysia and Singapore. The Department of Justice (DOJ) disclosed the case in a press release, but it remains an allegation and Lui is presumed innocent until a verdict.\nThe grand jury indictment (the panel that decides whether a case goes to trial), filed on September 29 at the federal court in Los Angeles, yields flight dates, order numbers and airline names. Such details are rare in a case like this, and they let readers follow the servers step by step.\nBeijing has since announced export controls on 23 AI technologies, with models and agents in scope .\nOn October 10, two leakers then claimed that Nvidia is ending the RTX 5090 and reserving GB202 chips for RTX PRO , with no official confirmation.\nThe case file (case 2:26-cr-00618, Central District of California) contains three counts. The first is conspiracy to violate the Export Control Reform Act, the U.S. law on sensitive exports, together with the Export Administration Regulations (EAR), the implementing rules of the Commerce Department. The second is smuggling out of the country, meaning help in getting goods to leave in violation of those rules. The third is conspiracy to commit money laundering.\nThe maximum penalty is 20 years on the first count and 10 on the second, with another 20 on the third. The 50-year sum that appears in some outlets is a theoretical ceiling, because it ignores how a judge combines sentences, while To…",
  "statement": "two leakers then claimed that Nvidia is ending the RTX 5090 and reserving GB202 chips for RTX PRO , with no official confirmation"
 },
 {
  "id": "G396",
  "article_id": "gnews:2957e5c79a365952140c8cce",
  "title": "DeepSeek develops AI chips, challenging Nvidia and Huawei",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-07",
  "article_opening": "DeepSeek develops AI chips, challenging Nvidia and Huawei\nJAKARTA - Chinese artificial intelligence (AI) startup DeepSeek is developing its own AI chips to reduce its reliance on products from Nvidia and Huawei, which have so far been used in the development of the company's AI models.\nAccording to Reuters , citing three sources familiar with the plans, DeepSeek's chips are being designed specifically for inference, the stage where trained AI models generate responses for users.\nUnlike chips use",
  "passage": "…chip design firms, foundries and memory suppliers to support the development of the semiconductors.\nDeepSeek has also increased recruitment of chip design engineers in recent months. The hiring process has been conducted discreetly, without publicly posting job openings on general recruitment platforms.\nDespite becoming one of the symbols of China's AI resurgence, DeepSeek has maintained a low profile and has not responded to Reuters' request for confirmation regarding the project.\nGlobally, DeepSeek's move follows a broader trend among major AI companies developing their own chips. OpenAI introduced its first inference chip, called Jalapeno, last month in collaboration with Broadcom, while Anthropic is also reportedly considering developing its own AI chips.\nFor DeepSeek, chip development carries greater strategic importance because US export restrictions have prevented Chinese companies from accessing Nvidia's most advanced AI chips.\nThe Chinese government has also continued to encourage domestic technology companies to build their own semiconductor supply chains.\nDeepSeek founder Liang Wenfeng acknowledged in a 2024 interview that chip export restrictions posed a challenge for the company.\nPreviously, DeepSeek used a combination of Nvidia and Huawei chips. The base model supporting DeepSeek R1 was trained using Nvidia's H800 chips, a product specifically designed for the Chinese market before the US government banned its export in late 2023.…",
  "statement": "Anthropic is also reportedly considering developing its own AI chips"
 },
 {
  "id": "G468",
  "article_id": "gnews:c6cdb0e2f11bbf941f9b8657",
  "title": "GeForce Trading Cards: Series 1 Celebrates The Generations Of GeForce",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-09",
  "article_opening": "By Richard Padilla\nEvery PC gamer has an origin story.\nMaybe it started with the first graphics card installed in a new desktop; a weekend LAN; a favorite arena shooter; a tech demo running on repeat; or perhaps a stunning new game that made an upgrade well and truly worthwhile.\nTo celebrate these moments, NVIDIA is introducing our first series of GeForce Trading Cards, a free collection featuring 14 possible designs showcasing some of GeForce PC gaming’s great moments. From iconic GPUs, memorab",
  "passage": "By Richard Padilla\nEvery PC gamer has an origin story.\nMaybe it started with the first graphics card installed in a new desktop; a weekend LAN; a favorite arena shooter; a tech demo running on repeat; or perhaps a stunning new game that made an upgrade well and truly worthwhile.\nTo celebrate these moments, NVIDIA is introducing our first series of GeForce Trading Cards, a free collection featuring 14 possible designs showcasing some of GeForce PC gaming’s great moments. From iconic GPUs, memorable demos, classic games, and callbacks to key moments in history for GeForce fans.\nFor generations of players, a GeForce graphics card has been more than a component: it’s been the upgrade or add-on that unlocked a new game, a new level of visual fidelity or a new way to experience a favorite world.\nSeries 1 looks back at that history through trading cards inspired by:\nNV1: The blueprint. Launched in 1995, NV1 was NVIDIA’s first mainstream multimedia processor, combining support for joystick, audio, VGA, 2D and 3D. It marked an early starting point for the company’s work in PC graphics.\nGeForce 256: The world’s f…",
  "statement": "NVIDIA is introducing our first series of GeForce Trading Cards, a free collection featuring 14 possible designs"
 },
 {
  "id": "G129",
  "article_id": "gnews:e4e5f1b5c0c68381e4c486e1",
  "title": "Here's What a $25,000 Investment in Nvidia Could Be Worth if History Repeats Itself",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-03",
  "article_opening": "The artificial intelligence chip giant Nvidia ( NVDA +2.48% ) has struggled recently and is now down nearly 13% over the past month (as of July 1). After years of an incredible AI rally, doubts are now creeping into investors' minds.\nThere are questions about whether the large tech companies can continue to invest hundreds of billions annually in AI infrastructure, which is driving much of the cycle, and whether constraints such as energy and power will eventually have a real impact. For Nvidia ",
  "passage": "… already has chips it claims are 15 times faster than some of Nvidia's models.\nAn even bigger threat would be if a competitor could challenge the operating system Nvidia has built for developers using its graphics processing units (GPUs) to train AI models, called Compute Unified Device Architecture (CUDA) .\nNvidia launched CUDA in 2006 and has built a whole ecosystem around it, so this is easier said than done, but it nonetheless represents a substantial part of Nvidia's moat and therefore would be devastating to the company if CUDA loses its grip on the market.\nAs you can see in the chart above, Nvidia has had an average forward price-to-earnings ratio of nearly 34 for the past two years. Wall Street consensus estimates suggest the company will earn adjusted earnings per share of $8.97 in its current fiscal year.\nApplying a 34 forward P/E, the stock would be worth roughly $305 per share, implying about 54% upside from current levels. This means a $25,000 investment in Nvidia would be worth over $38,400 if history repeats itself.\nNow, investors should understand that there's no guarantee this plays out. Nvidia, at its current market cap, is up against the law of large numbers. As companies get more mature, they typically face lower multiples. Competition could also be a real concern.\nHowever, Nvidia has traded at a 34 forward P/E before, so it could do so again should the company prove investors wrong about their concerns.\nHere's What a $25,000 Investment in Nvidia Could Be Worth if History Repeats …",
  "statement": "Wall Street consensus estimates suggest the company will earn adjusted earnings per share of $8.97 in its current fiscal year"
 },
 {
  "id": "G064",
  "article_id": "edgar:NVDA:0001045810-26-000060:nvda-20260628.htm",
  "title": "NVDA 8-K 8-K",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-02",
  "article_opening": "nvda-20260628\n0001045810 2026-06-28 2026-06-28\nDate of Report (Date of earliest event reported): June 28, 2026\nOn June 28, 2026, Ajay K. Puri, Executive Vice President, Worldwide Field Operations of NVIDIA Corporation, or the Company, notified the Company of his intention to retire from his role effective upon the employment commencement date of his successor. Mr. Puri, a 21-year executive at NVIDIA, is expected to remain with the Company in a senior advisory role to ensure a seamless transition",
  "passage": "…icted stock units, or RSUs, with a target value of $35,000,000, vesting approximately over a four-year period according to the Company’s standard new hire vesting schedule, and (b) multi-year performance-based RSUs, or MY PSUs, with a target value of $5,000,000, which will be eligible to vest based on the Company’s total shareholder return relative to the Standard & Poor’s 500 Composite Index over a three-year period. The terms of the RSUs and MY PSUs will be governed by the Company's Amended and Restated 2007 Equity Incentive Plan and applicable RSU and MY PSU agreements. Mr. Parker will be eligible to participate in the Company's benefit programs available to similarly situated employees.\nMr. Parker will enter into the Company’s indemnity agreement, which would require the Company to indemnify Mr. Parker, under the circumstances and to the extent provided for therein, against certain expenses and other amounts incurred by Mr. Parker as a result of being made a party to certain actions, suits, proceedings and the like by reason of his position as an officer of the Company.\nDate: July 2, 2026",
  "statement": "Mr. Parker will enter into the Company’s indemnity agreement"
 },
 {
  "id": "G493",
  "article_id": "gnews:8ca8a3d62e9d4663c70e7935",
  "title": "Elon Musk Makes Massive Nvidia Move at SpaceX",
  "publication_day": "2026-09-25",
  "article_opening": "Elon Musk is preparing another massive expansion of AI computing capacity, saying xAI's Colossus 2 cluster could more than double its current number of Nvidia chips by year-end. The timetable underscores just how aggressively Musk is scaling AI infrastructure across his businesses and gives Nvidia investors another sign that demand for its newest accelerators remains enormous.\nColossus 2 currently runs 110,000 Nvidia GB200 chips and 440,000 GB300s, according to Musk.\nAnother 220,000 GB300s are e",
  "passage": "Elon Musk is preparing another massive expansion of AI computing capacity, saying xAI's Colossus 2 cluster could more than double its current number of Nvidia chips by year-end. The timetable underscores just how aggressively Musk is scaling AI infrastructure across his businesses and gives Nvidia investors another sign that demand for its newest accelerators remains enormous.\nColossus 2 currently runs 110,000 Nvidia GB200 chips and 440,000 GB300s, according to Musk.\nAnother 220,000 GB300s are expected to be fully operational next week, followed by another 220,000 in November.\nMusk added that a further 220,000 GB300s could come online by late December if we get lucky.\nThat would bring Colossus 2 to roughly 1.21 million Nvidia GB200 and GB300 chips if every planned tranche arrives on schedule, more than double the 550,000 chips Musk said are currently installed.\nThe buildout is especially notable because xAI is already operating Colossus 1 with 150,000 Nvidia H100s, 50,000 H200s and 30,000 GB200s.\nMusk's companies are racing to secure enough compute for increasingly demanding AI models, putting xAI among the industry's most aggressive infrastructure buil…",
  "statement": "Colossus 2 currently runs 110,000 Nvidia GB200 chips and 440,000 GB300s, according to Musk."
 },
 {
  "id": "G211",
  "article_id": "nvblog:open-models-icml-2026",
  "title": "How Open Models Are Driving AI Research | NVIDIA Blog",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-06",
  "article_opening": "At the International Conference on Machine Learning, nearly 145 papers cite NVIDIA Nemotron open models and datasets as the foundation for new research.\nEvery year, the International Conference on Machine Learning (ICML) reveals where thousands of AI researchers have decided to put their work.\nThis year’s accepted papers reveal a clear direction: open frontier models and open AI infrastructure have become foundational to how modern AI science gets done.\nNVIDIA had 74 papers accepted at ICML 2026",
  "passage": "…ts gives researchers a reproducible foundation for training data curation . SDG tools enable creating high-quality training sets at a scale and speed that would’ve been impractical just a few years ago.\nThe Cosmos 3 family of open, frontier omnimodels gives researchers and developers a generational leap in the ability to build robots, autonomous vehicles and vision AI that perceive, reason, plan and act in the physical world.\nIn addition, the NVIDIA Alpamayo open model family for autonomous vehicle development, NVIDIA Isaac GR00T for robotics and NVIDIA BioNeMo for biomedical help accelerate research and development across industries.\nThe momentum extends beyond NVIDIA’s own research labs .\nBasecamp Research developed a new DNA foundation model, EDEN , that helps researchers interpret and design genetic sequences.\nMerck & Co. , uses KERMT to predict how potential drug molecules may behave in the body, including whether they are likely to be effective, safe and developable.\nSakana AI — attending ICML this year — built its Fugu and Fugu-Ultra models directly on Nemotron 3 Ultra, using the open foundation to push forward its work on AI research automation.\nKiloCode integrated Nemotron into its code-routing architecture, reporting token cost reductions of up to 90% — a result with real implications for the economics of deploying AI in production.\nNAVER developed its own model using the Nemotron architecture, extending the foundation for Korean-language AI research.\nTogether AI is hosting Nemotron models …",
  "statement": "Basecamp Research developed a new DNA foundation model, EDEN , that helps researchers interpret and design genetic sequences."
 },
 {
  "id": "G534",
  "article_id": "gnews:dbff79c9778e4b3d8ac8eef2",
  "title": "Nokia advances AI-RAN toward commercial deployment and physical AI, launches sovereign agentic AI platform for telco autonomy with NVIDIA",
  "publication_day": "2026-10-21",
  "article_opening": "Nokia advances AI-RAN toward commercial deployment and physical AI, launches sovereign agentic AI platform for telco autonomy with NVIDIA\nNew capabilities improve radio performance, enable real-time intelligence at the edge and extend governed AI agents across network operations.\nNokia AI-native anyRAN software and NVIDIA accelerated computing turn the radio access network into a distributed platform for sensing, intelligence and real-time action.\nBT, Deutsche Telekom, Elisa and Orange provide g",
  "passage": "…ive network environment. Distributed deployment and the evaluation of customer use cases and commercial AI-RAN services are expected in 2027.\nBT is preparing to deploy a live-network trial in Birmingham in 2027.\nDeutsche Telekom and Nokia are developing AI-based channel estimation as part of a broader set of AI-driven capabilities to improve network performance and efficiency. While this is just one element of the broader AI-RAN opportunity, initial laboratory measurements showed typical uplink throughput improvements of 10–15%, with greater gains under more complex radio conditions. The next phase will extend this work with additional AI spectral-efficiency algorithms and field validation.\nOrange plans to progress on testing AI-RAN in both lab and real network conditions.\nTogether, these customer programs are part of a broader platform built around three capabilities: AI-led spectral efficiency to extract more capacity from existing spectrum; integrated sensing and communications to help the network understand the physical environment; and localized inference to process intelligence close to where data is generated.\nAn open ecosystem for AI-RAN applications and deployment\nIn addition to its work with telecommunications providers, Nokia is extending its AI-RAN ecosystem through distributed applications (dApps) that run at the network edge and enable new services. Nokia’s E3 interface gives these applications real-time access to RAN data, turning the radio net…",
  "statement": "Orange plans to progress on testing AI-RAN in both lab and real network conditions."
 },
 {
  "id": "G379",
  "article_id": "gnews:295fceb25273abae9bb6c995",
  "title": "A Circuit Board Problem Just Delayed Nvidia's Next AI System to 2028 And Chip Stocks Are Already Feeling the Fallout",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-07",
  "article_opening": "A Circuit Board Problem Just Delayed Nvidia's Next AI System to 2028 And Chip Stocks Are Already Feeling the Fallout - 24/7 Wall St.\nA Circuit Board Problem Just Delayed Nvidia’s Next AI System to 2028 And Chip Stocks Are Already Feeling the Fallout\nA printed circuit board just pushed NVIDIA’s most powerful AI system into 2028 — at least according to one research firm. Semiconductor research firm SemiAnalysis reported on July 5–6, 2026 that NVIDIA’s Kyber NVL144 rack-scale AI system has been del",
  "passage": "… is strengthening, with leading customer forecasts exceeding our initial expectations.”\nBroadcom ( NASDAQ:AVGO ) benefits through custom ASICs. Q2 AI semiconductor revenue was $10.8 billion, +143% YoY, with Q3 guided to $16.0 billion. Bloomberg Intelligence forecasts 27% CAGR for the custom ASIC market through 2033.\nGoogle ( NASDAQ:GOOGL ) is both a Rubin customer and a TPU competitor. Google Cloud revenue rose 63% with backlog nearly doubling to over $460 billion, and 2026 capex is guided up tp $190 billion. A Kyber slip strengthens the in-house silicon case.\nNVIDIA has not confirmed the delay. The affected product is a 2028 item; Blackwell and current Rubin systems remain in short supply. NVIDIA still commands approximately 81% of the AI chip market , and GuruFocus analysis flags NVDA as 45% undervalued relative to GF Value at current prices. Hyperscalers cannot swap vendors overnight.\nI've spent more than 15 years inside enterprise software, working alongside the finance, sales operations, and HR leaders who run the revenue engines at some of the largest tech companies in the country.\nMy day job is helping enterprise executives make smarter decisions about retention, compensation, and growth. These are the same operational levers that show up in every earnings report investors actually read. That perspective shapes my writing for 24/7 Wall St.\nThe headline numbers are easy. The interesting stuff is underneath: how companies make money…",
  "statement": "NVIDIA still commands approximately 81% of the AI chip market"
 },
 {
  "id": "G349",
  "article_id": "gnews:f8191f5bb960d79e47c73d6c",
  "title": "Nvidia Stock Is Down 17% From Its High. Is the Artificial Intelligence (AI) Leader Finally Cheap?",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-07",
  "article_opening": "The largest company in the world, Nvidia (NASDAQ: NVDA), has had a bit of a rough stretch in recent months. Its stock peaked in May, but declined around 17% since then. That's not an insignificant decline, and leaves investors wondering when the next rally could be coming.\nI think there are some catalysts later on in July that could cause Nvidia's stock to rocket back to all-time highs, and investors would be smart to load up on shares before the rally occurs.\nThe next month will prove the AI bu",
  "passage": "…here are some catalysts later on in July that could cause Nvidia's stock to rocket back to all-time highs, and investors would be smart to load up on shares before the rally occurs.\nThe next month will prove the AI build-out is as strong as ever\nNvidia makes graphics processing units (GPUs) along with various products to support its accelerated computing ecosystem. GPUs are still the top choice for running AI workloads, and Nvidia dominates the market share of GPUs sold for data center computing. There's a very strong correlation between increasing data center spending and Nvidia's sales, and that trend will likely accelerate as data centers shift from construction costs to computing costs.\nThis year, the big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures . Next year, Nvidia claims this group will spend more than $1 trillion.\nThat's a major increase and should boost Nvidia's revenue along the way. In July, all of the big four AI hyperscalers report earnings, which will likely include commentary surrounding AI spending and how these companies are monetizing AI. I'll be paying attention to all of them, but the biggest one I'll be watching is Microsoft (NASDAQ: MSFT), as its fiscal year ended on June 30. Microsoft will provide investors with fiscal year (FY) 2027 capital expenditures guidance, and a huge jump over last year's figure could confirm the bull case behind Nvidia's stock. That could help propel it back to all-time highs, which is why I think Nvidia is a strong buy now.\nAnother key announcement will come …",
  "statement": "This year, the big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures . Next year, Nvidia claims this group will spend more than $1 trillion."
 },
 {
  "id": "G245",
  "article_id": "gnews:25d0b1d2a9dfe0bb99432e1a",
  "title": "NVIDIA Falls 7% in a Month: Should You Still Hold NVDA Stock or Exit?",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-06",
  "article_opening": "/ NVIDIA Falls 7% in a Month: Should You Still Hold NVDA Stock or Exit?\nNVIDIA Falls 7% in a Month: Should You Still Hold NVDA Stock or Exit?\nNVIDIA Corporation NVDA shares have declined 6.6% over the past month, underperforming the broader Zacks Computer and Technology sector, which slipped 2.2% during the same period. At first glance, the decline may look worrying, but a closer look suggests the weakness is more about market sentiment than company-specific problems.\nThe sell-off has been wides",
  "passage": "…ion is equally impressive. During the first quarter, NVIDIA generated $50.3 billion in operating cash flow and $48.6 billion in free cash flow. It ended the quarter with $80.6 billion in cash, cash equivalents and marketable securities, up from $62.6 billion in the previous quarter.\nThis enormous financial flexibility allows NVIDIA to invest aggressively in research and development, expand production capacity and reward shareholders. During the quarter, the company repurchased $19.3 billion worth of shares and paid $243 million in dividends.\nNVDA Stock’s Valuation Still Looks Attractive\nDespite becoming one of the world's most valuable companies, NVIDIA's valuation is still very attractive.\nThe stock currently trades at a forward 12-month price-to-earnings (P/E) multiple of 18.87, below the Zacks Computer & Technology sector average of 22.73.\nNVIDIA Forward 12-Month P/E Ratio\nNVIDIA also trades at a lower multiple than several semiconductor peers such as Marvell Technology, STMicroelectronics and Broadcom. Marvell carries a forward P/E of 49.59, STMicroelectronics trades at 33.49, and Broadcom at 21.50.\nThat discount is noteworthy because NVIDIA continues to deliver faster growth, stronger profitability and a more dominant competitive position than many of its peers. If the company maintains its current execution, the valuation still leaves room for long-term upside.\nShould Investors Hold NVIDIA Stock?\nThe recent pullback appears to be driven more by broad semiconductor weakness and concerns over AI spending than by any deterior…",
  "statement": "The stock currently trades at a forward 12-month price-to-earnings (P/E) multiple of 18.87, below the Zacks Computer & Technology sector average of 22.73."
 },
 {
  "id": "G287",
  "article_id": "gnews:795ea94e1100415a67fd1a96",
  "title": "Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 reportedly pushed back more than a year, Asian suppliers drop",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-06",
  "article_opening": "Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 reportedly pushed back more than a year, Asian suppliers drop\nA report from analyst firm SemiAnalysis says Nvidia's next-generation AI server rack has been delayed by more than a year. Suppliers across Japan, Taiwan, South Korea, and Hong Kong saw their stock prices drop sharply in response.\nAsian tech stocks slid after analyst firm SemiAnalysis reported on X that Nvidia's next AI server rack system, Kyber NVL144, has been pushed back more than twelve months to 2028 because",
  "passage": "…s largest customer, dropped as much as ten percent. Kingboard Laminates fell 18 percent in Hong Kong, Elite Material lost ten percent in Taiwan, and Samsung Electro-Mechanics slid eleven percent in South Korea. The sell-off follows massive run-ups. Samsung Electro-Mechanics had gained more than 600 percent this year, and Kingboard Laminates more than 470 percent.\nScrapped designs give competitors an opening\nSemiAnalysis lists more setbacks. A planned alternative design called NVL72x2, which would have placed two Oberon racks back to back, has been scrapped entirely. Cloud providers and large data center operators pushed back against the unusual form factor and the high operational overhead. The more powerful version of the upcoming Rubin Ultra chip with four compute dies has also been canceled. Only the smaller two-die version remains, delivering roughly half the real-world compute performance.\nA key interconnect technology called CPO-NVSwitch, which links many chips into a single large system, won't arrive until the generation after next, called Feynman. That means Nvidia lacks a proven way to scale Rubin Ultra to very large systems for now. The gap could give competitors like AMD's MI500X or Google's TPUv8i Broadfly room to move in. Nvidia plans to make up for the shortfall by selling more Oberon-Rubin racks in the existing form factor.\nA Kyber delay doesn't necessarily mean overall AI spending will shrink, said Gary Tan of Allspring Global Investments, according to Bloomberg . It shows that Nvidia's most ambitious system is taking longer than expected. The current stock weaknes…",
  "statement": "The more powerful version of the upcoming Rubin Ultra chip with four compute dies has also been canceled. Only the smaller two-die version remains, delivering roughly half the real-world compute performance."
 },
 {
  "id": "G350",
  "article_id": "gnews:f8191f5bb960d79e47c73d6c",
  "title": "Nvidia Stock Is Down 17% From Its High. Is the Artificial Intelligence (AI) Leader Finally Cheap?",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-07",
  "article_opening": "The largest company in the world, Nvidia (NASDAQ: NVDA), has had a bit of a rough stretch in recent months. Its stock peaked in May, but declined around 17% since then. That's not an insignificant decline, and leaves investors wondering when the next rally could be coming.\nI think there are some catalysts later on in July that could cause Nvidia's stock to rocket back to all-time highs, and investors would be smart to load up on shares before the rally occurs.\nThe next month will prove the AI bu",
  "passage": "…lp propel it back to all-time highs, which is why I think Nvidia is a strong buy now.\nAnother key announcement will come from Taiwan Semiconductor Manufacturing Company (NYSE: TSM), Nvidia's primary logic chip fabricator. If TSMC reports strong growth from AI semiconductor sales, it will be easy to draw a conclusion that Nvidia is also doing well.\nI think both of these companies will report strong earnings with great forward guidance, and that could cause Nvidia's stock to rise. Even if it doesn't, Nvidia reports earnings in late August and will likely report another blowout quarter, as there hasn't been any shift in actual spending habits from Nvidia's core clients yet.\nAs for a price tag, Nvidia trades for just 21.7 times forward earnings -- the same price tag as the S&P 500 (SNPINDEX: ^GSPC).",
  "statement": "Nvidia trades for just 21.7 times forward earnings"
 },
 {
  "id": "G224",
  "article_id": "gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6",
  "title": "Nvidia GPU Debt Backstop Unleashes the AI Project Trinity: Capital, Offtake and Datacenters",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-06",
  "article_opening": "Up until now the majority of AI buildouts have been primarily cashflow funded by the hyperscalers such as Google, Amazon, Meta, Microsoft, Oracle. Over the last year, that's started to turn with Oracle then Meta, and now even Google turning to debt. Nvidia revenue is skyrocketing, and even 3 years into the build out, the general market is still materially lower on shipment volumes and revenue estimates for Nvidia in the 2nd half of this year versus our through supply chain tracking in the Accele",
  "passage": "…dia is clearly showing it stands ready to offer this support as the central bank.\nNvidia’s backstop program is typically six years in length, and during these six years, Nvidia stands ready to purchase compute at pre-agreed price levels that vary over the time period. Each Neocloud is expected to negotiate the backstop terms individually, and we can expect that different Neoclouds may end up with different revenue share and backstop schedules. The below table is an illustrative example to explain the basic terms of the program – we present a backstop pricing curve that we believe to be on the lower end of the backstop range – in this case at an average of $2.36 over the six-year period, but we expect that most Neoclouds will negotiate higher backstops.\nLet’s start by exploring Neocloud economics under the backstop program by considering a few different scenarios. In the first scenario, the Neocloud focuses on a customer base that rents at a 1-year or lower tenor, so we model the 1-year rental price for a GB300 starting at $6.75/hr for the first year before decaying over time as the overall market cost of compute declines. As with any business that is operating a “curve trade” – that is investing long and renting out on shorter tenors – we should expect that in theory the average realized rental price over the project lifetime should be slightly higher than the current 6y fixed price of ~$4.00. This is to compensate the Neocloud for takin…",
  "statement": "we expect that most Neoclouds will negotiate higher backstops"
 },
 {
  "id": "G303",
  "article_id": "gnews:417f6244f0aaa27123023deb",
  "title": "A Utah microreactor just powered an Nvidia AI chip live on stage, a first for next-generation nuclear in America, and the two companies used the moment to announce a 30-megawatt AI factory in the desert designed to drink zero local water",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-06",
  "article_opening": "Valar Atomics just made history in the Utah desert by connecting a next-generation nuclear reactor directly to an Nvidia AI chip — a first in America — and the implications for the data center industry's biggest PR crisis go far beyond one desktop PC drawing power on a stage.\nThe reactor cooling itself with pressurized helium instead of water is the quiet technical detail that changes everything about where AI infrastructure can be built next.\nWith over $130 billion in data center projects alrea",
  "passage": "…ventional 30 MW site and you land around 78 million gallons a year. The pitch here is zero of it, in a desert.\n“Through this work with Valar Atomics, Nvidia is exploring how behind-the-meter, waterless advanced nuclear systems could support future AI factories,” John Josephakis, a global vice president at Nvidia, told Reuters.\nNote the verb. Exploring. What nobody has published is a construction schedule, an investment figure, or a permit. FOX 13 reported the future Emery County factory drew big cheers at the event, and has no timeline attached.\nValar itself is a 2023-founded outfit from El Segundo, California, reportedly backed by Oculus founder Palmer Luckey and Palantir CTO Shyam Sankar, with CB Insights data putting its valuation around $2 billion as of March. Money is not the bottleneck here. Licenses are.\nNone of this lands in a vacuum. Utah spent the spring arguing over Kevin O’Leary’s Stratos data center project in Box Elder County, with public pushback centered on the Great Salt Lake, and Governor Spencer Cox responded with an executive order putting guardrails on data center development.\nEmy Lesofski, who runs the state’s Office of Energy Development, told FOX 13 the position is blunt: use the resources responsibly, or the state won’t let you build.\nWard 250 sits in Emery County coal country on purpose, a proving ground pitched at the heart of Cox’s Operation Gigawatt plan to double state power production within a decade. Utah already host…",
  "statement": "with CB Insights data putting its valuation around $2 billion as of March"
 },
 {
  "id": "G334",
  "article_id": "gnews:b48593184704bbbe6eddbc03",
  "title": "As chip sector takes it on the chin, traders bet on a big Nvidia rally",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-07",
  "article_opening": "Is tech's biggest sleeping giant about to wake up?\nShares of Nvidia fought into the green despite a 5% sell-off in chip stocks tracked by the VanEck Semiconductor ETF (SMH) , a notable outperformance a day after a research report claiming the AI leader was at least a year behind on manufacturing of its next line of server racks.\nThe stock's trading just under $200, a level it's flirted with since late last month, down 17% from its May record and up just 4% on the year, as investors have turned t",
  "passage": "Is tech's biggest sleeping giant about to wake up?\nShares of Nvidia fought into the green despite a 5% sell-off in chip stocks tracked by the VanEck Semiconductor ETF (SMH) , a notable outperformance a day after a research report claiming the AI leader was at least a year behind on manufacturing of its next line of server racks.\nThe stock's trading just under $200, a level it's flirted with since late last month, down 17% from its May record and up just 4% on the year, as investors have turned their attention to other AI components, like the memory-makers.\nOne thing Nvidia has those stocks don't right now: bullish options flows.\nMore than 1.5 million calls traded in Nvidia Tuesday, compared to under 690,000 puts, with more than twice as many calls bought versus puts bought, according to ThinkorSwim data. Volume ratios are about the opposite in the sector ETF, with puts outpacing calls almost four-to-one in SMH, with traders buying 33,000 puts, compared to just 7,300 calls.\nSimilar action took place in Nvidia Monda…",
  "statement": "a research report claiming the AI leader was at least a year behind on manufacturing of its next line of server racks"
 },
 {
  "id": "G124",
  "article_id": "gnews:e4e5f1b5c0c68381e4c486e1",
  "title": "Here's What a $25,000 Investment in Nvidia Could Be Worth if History Repeats Itself",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-03",
  "article_opening": "The artificial intelligence chip giant Nvidia ( NVDA +2.48% ) has struggled recently and is now down nearly 13% over the past month (as of July 1). After years of an incredible AI rally, doubts are now creeping into investors' minds.\nThere are questions about whether the large tech companies can continue to invest hundreds of billions annually in AI infrastructure, which is driving much of the cycle, and whether constraints such as energy and power will eventually have a real impact. For Nvidia ",
  "passage": "… questions about whether the large tech companies can continue to invest hundreds of billions annually in AI infrastructure, which is driving much of the cycle, and whether constraints such as energy and power will eventually have a real impact. For Nvidia specifically, competition in the chip space seems to be intensifying.\nThat said, Nvidia hasn't done anything to suggest its business is at risk of slowing, so if the concerns don't materialize, the stock could have upside. Here's what a $25,000 investment in Nvidia could be worth if history repeats itself.\nWhile many high-flying AI companies trade at monster valuations, that's not really something you can say about Nvidia.\nOn the surface, Nvidia trading at roughly 22 times forward earnings looks quite reasonable. In the first quarter of Nvidia's fiscal year 2027, ending April 26, the company grew revenue 85% year over year, while diluted earnings per share soared by 140%.\nIn March, CEO Jensen Huang said he expects the company's Blackwell and Vera Rubin chips to generate $1 trillion in sales between that time and the end of 2027.\nFurthermore, in its first fiscal quarter of 2027, Nvidia announced plans to become much more involved in the central processing unit (CPU) space , which has been in high demand due to the rise of agentic AI. Management said they think they can generate nearly $20 billion of CPU sales, making it an instant industry leader.\nObviously, there are real con…",
  "statement": "Nvidia trading at roughly 22 times forward earnings"
 },
 {
  "id": "G454",
  "article_id": "gnews:07610e55de6cc86b2b508b1d",
  "title": "NVIDIA Nemotron Achieves Benchmark-Leading Performance With LangChain Deep Agents Harness",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-08",
  "article_opening": "Tuning the LangChain Deep Agents harness for NVIDIA Nemotron 3 Ultra delivers leading performance and faster task completion on an open stack that enterprises can run, customize and control.\nNVIDIA Nemotron 3 Ultra is offering leading performance at lower cost than top closed models with the largest and most widely adopted AI agent orchestration platform.\nLangChain tuned its Deep Agents harness for NVIDIA Nemotron 3 Ultra, achieving the highest accuracy among open models, while completing more t",
  "passage": "…ng its Deep Agents harness specifically for NVIDIA Nemotron 3 Ultra, it allows for high-performing agents that complete more tasks, run faster and give enterprises a fully open stack they can customize, own and run anywhere.\n“The way to build better agents is to keep improving the system around the model,” said Harrison Chase, cofounder and CEO of LangChain. “Memory, tool use, evaluation and model behavior compound when teams can tune them together. Our work with NVIDIA shows that enterprises can get strong performance from an open stack while keeping control over the agent systems they are building.”\nAbridge, Amdocs and Box are embedding specialized agents directly into their platforms and global systems integrator EY is expanding its NVIDIA implementation capabilities around NVIDIA NemoClaw blueprints for LangChain Deep Agents, helping clients customize, evaluate and govern specialized agents across high-value workflows.\nNVIDIA founder and CEO Jensen Huang recently sat down with Chase to discuss why the last six months have seen a leap in useful AI for enterprises.\nLangChain’s team ran Nemotron 3 Ultra against its public Deep Agents benchmark suite, then analyzed the deep agent’s execution traces to find exactly where it lost points. Instead of retraining the model, the team tuned the harness around it — adjusting system prompts, tool descriptions and middleware.\nEvery developer using LangChain Deep Agents with Nemotron 3 Ultra can put this to work today — the tuned profile is available directly through LangChain…",
  "statement": "global systems integrator EY is expanding its NVIDIA implementation capabilities around NVIDIA NemoClaw blueprints for LangChain Deep Agents"
 },
 {
  "id": "G060",
  "article_id": "gnews:3854aeddaa3f31de00cf2816",
  "title": "Nvidia offers to take a cut of AI cloud revenue on top of hardware sales in new optional financing vehicle — trades tokens for revenue cut",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-02",
  "article_opening": "Nvidia offers to take a cut of AI cloud revenue on top of hardware sales in new optional financing vehicle — trades tokens for revenue cut\nBy Luke James\nSharon AI and Firmus are the first partners, with a combined total of up to 210,000 GPUs.\nNvidia has announced a new business model under which it’ll be able to double-dip for revenue on the same silicon: once when its partner AI clouds use its hardware, and again as an ongoing percentage of the revenue that hardware generates. In a blog post co",
  "passage": "…\nIn an 8-K filing dated June 12th, Sharon AI disclosed that the agreement runs for six years and covers 72 MW of new Australian data center capacity built to Nvidia's DSX AI factory design, scaling to as many as 40,000 Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs. The Nasdaq-listed neocloud separately holds a revenue-share facility of up to $200 million with investor Digital Alpha, disclosed in its CY25 results, meaning portions of its income are now pledged in two directions. Meanwhile, Firmus is building a DSX-aligned campus in Batam, Indonesia, that’s expected to scale to 360 MW and house up to 170,000 Nvidia GPUs.\nNvidia has spent much of the last year funnelling cash directly to its customers, including a $30 billion participation in OpenAI's $110 billion funding round and backing for xAI's $20 billion Colossus 2 financing , arrangements that drew repeated circular financing criticism. The new model inverts that: rather than investing capital that returns as GPU orders, Nvidia extends credit support and collects a royalty on its partners’ sales for years afterward.\nThat royalty also ties a slice of Nvidia's income to utilization instead of hardware sales. If partner clouds can’t keep racks rented, the usage-linked stream shrinks, a live concern given the depreciation pressure already building on operators paying off hardware that Nvidia refreshes pretty much every year.\nRevenue? All these startups are or will be horribly unprofitable, the lengths one take…",
  "statement": "a $30 billion participation in OpenAI's $110 billion funding round"
 },
 {
  "id": "G179",
  "article_id": "gnews:552f8c2bdfd080555d592587",
  "title": "Nvidia's next-gen AI rack system delayed to 2028 on manufacturing snags, SemiAnalysis says",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-05",
  "article_opening": "Jensen Huang, chief executive officer of Nvidia Corp., speaks next to a Vera Rubin Ultra Kyber compute tray and a Vera Rubin Ultra Kyber NVLink midplane during a keynote address at the Nvidia GTC conference in San Jose, California, March 16, 2026.\nNvidia 's next marquee product — the Kyber rack-scale architecture designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028, according to research firm SemiAnalysis, the latest in a string of reported setbacks rais",
  "passage": "…ildouts,\" said Paul Triolo, a partner at consultancy DGA-Albright Stonebridge Group, noting the company \"has faced these kinds of challenges before, and has worked with vendors to overcome technical issues.\"\nWith power supply likely to remain the major constraint on American AI data center spending, Triolo said, \"delays in getting to more advanced systems could just mean that the new systems are ready by the time the U.S. can work to overcome some of the critical bottlenecks on power now dogging the industry.\"\nNvidia's current-generation Rubin systems are in full production and begin shipping this fall to eight cloud partners, including Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure and Google Cloud. SemiAnalysis also projects Nvidia's data-center compute revenue will run 20% above Wall Street consensus in the second half of fiscal 2027.\nWhile Huawei and other domestic manufacturers \"will potentially gain some time,\" China's hardware ecosystem is increasingly diverging from — rather than chasing — the Nvidia-led model, Triolo said. \"The issue is no longer really catching up, but how good will China's alternative AI stack be by 2030,\" he said.",
  "statement": "SemiAnalysis also projects Nvidia's data-center compute revenue will run 20% above Wall Street consensus in the second half of fiscal 2027"
 },
 {
  "id": "G506",
  "article_id": "gnews:488fd6e4ba9177df77a2fed2",
  "title": "SpaceX Wants to Borrow $40 Billion to Buy Nvidia Chips",
  "publication_day": "2026-10-07",
  "article_opening": "SpaceX is borrowing $40 billion for NVIDIA chips while posting a $541 million quarterly loss on capex more than double its revenue.\nApollo Global Management is expected to lead the deal, as Morgan Stanley warns AI infrastructure will need $1.5 trillion in outside financing by 2028.\nLockup expirations will release up to 1.3 billion SpaceX shares after Q3 earnings, pressuring stock as insiders sell right after a post-IPO rally.\nA recent report citing people familiar with the matter said SpaceX ( N",
  "passage": "…DIA.\"\nBorrowing avoids diluting shareholders today. Even so, lockup expirations will add 328.4 million marketable shares on October 9, another 328.4 million on October 24 and up to 1.3 billion after third-quarter earnings.\nThe stock priced at $135 in its June 11, 2026 initial public offering, peaked at $225.64 and closed at $171.92 on October 6. That close came one session after a 7.63% gain to its highest close since June, so insiders got their first chance to sell right after a rally, as the debt was being marketed.\nNVIDIA closed at $239.24 after a record intraday high of $243.37, worth about $5.78 trillion. A large order matters, but at that size, no single customer changes the business.\nMorgan Stanley ( NYSE:MS ) estimates AI infrastructure will need $1.5 trillion of outside financing by 2028. When more buyers borrow to pay for chips, NVIDIA's revenue stability depends in part on whether those buyers can carry their debt.\nAnalysts are bullish. Adam Jonas carries a $300 target, Eric Sheridan of Goldman Sachs ( NYSE:GS ) is at $230, and the average is near $235. Those targets assume the spending pays off, and lenders are now being asked to fund that same plan with debt.\nAt a market value of about $2.2 trillion to $2.3 trillion, with losses, heavy borrowing, and new share supply, SpaceX carries risks that the analyst targets may not fully reflect.\nNVIDIA's exposure to the same AI spending carries less balance-sheet risk, because it gets paid when the chips ship, while SpaceX carrie…",
  "statement": "Morgan Stanley ( NYSE:MS ) estimates AI infrastructure will need $1.5 trillion of outside financing by 2028"
 },
 {
  "id": "G527",
  "article_id": "gnews:b5615714b8f92f78c23233c7",
  "title": "Apple, Nvidia lead list of tech companies that donated to Trump’s White House ballroom project",
  "publication_day": "2026-10-16",
  "article_opening": "Some of the biggest tech companies donated tens of millions of dollars for the construction of the White House ballroom.\nBy Matt Binder\nMatt Binder joined Mashable's tech vertical in 2018, where he covers social media, tech policy, cybersecurity, online scams, cryptocurrency, AI, creator news, weird tech, and other related tech beats.\nHere are the tech companies that gave millions to Donald Trump's White House ballroom – and just how much they donated..\nSome of the world's biggest tech companies",
  "passage": "…les Ergen, the co-founder of Dish Network.\nHowever, Apple and Nvidia were far from the only tech companies to make massive donations for the ballroom.\nGoogle provided $22 million, via a settlement the company made with Trump related to the shutdown of his YouTube channel following the events of Jan. 6, as Mashable reported previously.\nMicrosoft donated $10 million, and Palantir was right behind them with a near $10 million donation of their own.\nAmazon, Meta, and T-Mobile donated $5 million each.\nA number of cryptocurrency companies like Coinbase, Ripple, and the Winklevoss twins, who represent their Gemini crypto exchange, also donated $5 million. Crypto exchange Kraken donated $1 million.\nHP and Micron each gave the president $2.5 million and Salesforce donated $2 million.\nNon-tech companies like defense contractors and the former CEO of Marvel's foundation also gave tens of millions of dollars to the ballroom project.\nThe voluntary accord lays out four layers of safety controls, while leaving companies to decide how and whether to enforce them.\nBy Chance Townsend\n\"Isn't this bullroom of yours supposed to include a bunch of top secret military facilities, and if it does, maybe don't do the tour with the heads of foreign governments?\"\nBy Shannon Connellan\nBy Mashable Team\nMashable is a registered trademark of Ziff Davis and may not be used by third parties without express written permission.",
  "statement": "HP and Micron each gave the president $2.5 million and Salesforce donated $2 million."
 },
 {
  "id": "G048",
  "article_id": "gnews:d1364ee37c17c361ae74f563",
  "title": "Nvidia taps AI cloud providers to expand compute access for startups",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-02",
  "article_opening": "Two initial partners are offering potential access to more than 200,000 GPUs, including via an upcoming data center in Batam, Indonesia.\nChipmaker Nvidia is expanding its support for fast-growing AI startups by introducing a partnership program that leverages its network of AI cloud service providers to offer these firms access to critical computing infrastructure, the company said Thursday.\nCloud-based AI firms, model builders and other enterprises will share both product and cloud revenue with",
  "passage": "…re than 200,000 GPUs, including via an upcoming data center in Batam, Indonesia.\nChipmaker Nvidia is expanding its support for fast-growing AI startups by introducing a partnership program that leverages its network of AI cloud service providers to offer these firms access to critical computing infrastructure, the company said Thursday.\nCloud-based AI firms, model builders and other enterprises will share both product and cloud revenue with Nvidia, which is positioning itself as an intermediary helping startups gain direct access to full-stack computing powered by Nvidia chips.\nIn its announcement, Nvidia named two initial Australian firms that will provide the compute power for the scheme.\nSharon AI will deploy up to 40,000 Nvidia GPUs, while AI infrastructure company Firmus Technologies says it is building a data center in Batam, Indonesia, which is expected to scale to 360 megawatts and house up to 170,000 Nvidia GPUs.\nNvidia's move illustrates the critical importance of access to scarce compute power for AI-oriented startups, with GPUs likened to oil and even reportedly tied to futures contracts as users grapple with fluctuations in cost and issues around availability.\nMeanwhile, AI firms have increasingly entered into revenue and equity-sharing agreements with chipmakers in order to circumvent liquidity issues afflicting the sector.\nOpenAI has inked a number of deals that have seen it buy shares or entertain investmen…",
  "statement": "Sharon AI will deploy up to 40,000 Nvidia GPUs"
 },
 {
  "id": "G427",
