  "publication_day": "2026-07-10",
  "article_opening": "Here are the biggest calls on Wall Street on Friday: DA Davidson initiates Agco as buy DA says the ag equipment company has \"earnings leverage.\" \"We are initiating coverage of AGCO with a BUY rating and $160 price target.\" Wells Fargo initiates X4 Pharmaceuticals as overweight The firm said in its initiation of X4 that the Street is underestimating the probability of success of the company's trial of its immune deficiency drug. \"We believe Street's POS [probability of success] is too low for mav",
  "passage": "…to OW w/ a $1,100 PT (~20x CY28 P/E; was $900).\" Morgan Stanley reiterates SpaceX as overweight Morgan Stanley says the release of Grok 4.5 is an important catalyst for SpaceX. \"Key question remains whether SpaceXAI will be able to combine Grok + Cursor to create compelling, differentiated enterprise products that aren't easily replicated by competing labs.\" Morgan Stanley reiterates Nvidia as overweight Morgan Stanley says the stock remains a top idea following a meeting with management. \"This week, we hosted a number of investor meetings in California as part of an NDR with Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang, CFO Colette Kress, and head of IR Toshiya Hari. The meetings were upbeat, as expected, with the company describing \"accelerating growth rates\" even as revenues approach the $100 bn per quarter mark.\" Raymond James initiates BrightSpring as outperform Raymond James says the healthcare company has a differentiated offering. \"We are initiating coverage of BrightSpring Health Services (BTSG) with an Outperform rating and an $80 price target.\" Bernstein upgrades J.B. Hunt to outperform from market perform The firm says it's not too late to buy shares of the trucking company. \"We are upgrading JBHT to Outperform and raising our price target to $329. The stock has rallied on better end market conditions, and with earnings geared to follow through an eventual peak in truck rates we would rather be late than on the sidelines.\" Stifel upgrades Shopify to buy from hold Stifel says Shopify is a \"…",
  "statement": "the company describing \"accelerating growth rates\" even as revenues approach the $100 bn per quarter mark"
 },
 {
  "id": "G443",
  "article_id": "gnews:c675b7c2548b02233ee7b05c",
  "title": "TD Cowen Names Nvidia Stock a Top Pick on Massive Unpriced AI Upside",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-10",
  "article_opening": "Price change for Nvidia stock in last 1 year: 24%\n$NVDA Stock Price as of Jul. 9: $203\n52-Week High: $236\n$NVDA Stock Price Target: $302\nTD Cowen reiterated its Buy rating on Nvidia ( NVDA ) stock and named it a Top Pick, maintaining a $275 price target implying roughly 35% upside from where shares closed.\nThe call came after the firm hosted an investor lunch with Nvidia’s leadership team, including CEO Jensen Huang, CFO Colette Kress, and Head of Investor Relations Toshiya Hari.\nAnalyst Joshua ",
  "passage": "…mpany a real edge as AI keeps evolving from general chatbot-style applications toward more specialized, industry-specific tools.\nTo put some numbers behind that, Nvidia estimates about 20% of its business comes from supporting frontier AI models built by companies like OpenAI and Anthropic.\nMeanwhile, TD Cowen estimates that Nvidia’s revenue from enterprise applications across multiple industries is in the low-to-mid-teens range.\nThat’s a meaningful chunk of business coming from outside the flashy AI chatbot headlines, which is part of Buchalter’s point about Nvidia’s opportunity being underappreciated.\nTD Cowen also highlighted evidence that computing capacity is genuinely tight right now. Rental prices for older GPU models have been climbing, AI adoption continues to widen across industries, and recent cloud computing deals have been signed at premium prices.\nAll of that points to demand outpacing supply.\nNvidia’s own recent quarterly results back up the strength story.\nThe company posted $82 billion in total revenue, up 85% year-over-year, marking its third straight quarter of accelerating growth.\nData center revenue alone hit $75 billion, up 92% year over year.\nNvidia also highlighted a new growth driver: its Vera CPU line, which the company expects to generate $20 billion in sales by the end of this fiscal year, a notable push into a market historically dominated by Intel and AMD.\nBeyond the CPU expansion, Nvidia has been st…",
  "statement": "Rental prices for older GPU models have been climbing"
 },
 {
  "id": "G014",
  "article_id": "gnews:87607a311ad24b410c6ef85a",
  "title": "Intel Price Forecast: Nvidia Picked Xeon 6, Invested $5B, Yet Analysts Still Trail INTC",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-01",
  "article_opening": "Intel Price Forecast: Nvidia Picked Xeon 6, Invested $5B, Yet Analysts Still Trail INTC\nIntel Corporation is demonstrating significant momentum, trading at $140.05 amid robust AI-driven demand. Core catalysts include NVIDIA’s $5 billion equity investment and the selection of Intel’s Xeon 6 for DGX Rubin AI systems, alongside multi-year, volume-locked agreements with Google and Microsoft. Q1 2026 data shows strong revenue growth in Data Center/AI and Foundry segments, with AI-linked revenue reach",
  "passage": "Intel Price Forecast: Nvidia Picked Xeon 6, Invested $5B, Yet Analysts Still Trail INTC\nIntel Corporation is demonstrating significant momentum, trading at $140.05 amid robust AI-driven demand. Core catalysts include NVIDIA’s $5 billion equity investment and the selection of Intel’s Xeon 6 for DGX Rubin AI systems, alongside multi-year, volume-locked agreements with Google and Microsoft. Q1 2026 data shows strong revenue growth in Data Center/AI and Foundry segments, with AI-linked revenue reaching 60% of the mix. While currently trading above consensus analyst targets, the valuation is supported by evolving commercial fundamentals. Technically, the stock remains in a neutral-bullish trend, with a potential move toward $159.90 if it clears $150.40 resistance.\nTradingKey - Intel Corporation (NASDAQ: INTC ) sits at $140.05, holding firm on the ascending trendline within the 2H timef…",
  "statement": "Intel Corporation is demonstrating significant momentum, trading at $140.05 amid robust AI-driven demand."
 },
 {
  "id": "G274",
  "article_id": "gnews:4d1b1ca014068fb532f116b3",
  "title": "Nvidia’s Biggest Threat Is This: Everyone Is Desperate to Stop Paying Nvidia Prices.",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-06",
  "article_opening": "Nvidia’s Biggest Threat Is This: Everyone Is Desperate to Stop Paying Nvidia Prices.\nNVIDIA (NASDAQ:NVDA | NVDA Price Prediction) owns the AI compute market, but every major customer is spending billions to buy less of what it sells. NVIDIA carries a $4.75 trillion market cap and sits between a 52-week low of $158.18…\nBy Omor Ibne Ehsan\nNVIDIA posted 85% revenue growth to $82 billion in Q1, but half its data center sales come from hyperscalers actively funding custom AI chip alternatives.\nAmazon",
  "passage": "…is ramping, Rubin was announced, and Jensen Huang called the AI factory buildout “the largest infrastructure expansion in human history.” The dividend raised to $0.25 quarterly and an additional $80 billion buyback was authorized.\nAll of this says NVDA stock is set to keep delivering, as long as the broader market remains bullish.\nThe customer list is the threat. Amazon has disclosed Trainium is now a multi-billion-dollar business, and every hyperscaler funding NVIDIA’s data center segment also funds an alternative. Custom silicon “not only gives you a differentiation factor where you can be cheaper than competitors, but it also allows you to have some leverage over NVIDIA in negotiations.”\nChina data center compute revenue is effectively zero, and Colette Kress (Nvidia’s CFO) said losing that market, which NVIDIA sizes at “close to about $50 billion in the future,” would be material. Supply commitments of $119 billion compound demand risk if hyperscaler orders slow, and insiders have logged 16 recent transactions, net selling.\nNeither thesis has resolved. Blackwell ramps while Trainium and TPU volumes rise in parallel. NVIDIA’s NVLink Fusion strategy lets hyperscalers bolt custom accelerators onto NVIDIA’s fabric so the interconnect stays sticky even when compute does not.\nWatch hyperscaler capex mix, whether networking growth stays vertical, and any China SKU announcement over the next two quarters. Any one breaking hard could tip the call.\nNVIDIA trades at $196 against an analyst consensus target of ~$301.62 as of this writing, implying 53% upside. Coverage skews heav…",
  "statement": "China data center compute revenue is effectively zero, and Colette Kress (Nvidia’s CFO) said losing that market, which NVIDIA sizes at “close to about $50 billion in the future,” would be material."
 },
 {
  "id": "G272",
  "article_id": "gnews:4d1b1ca014068fb532f116b3",
  "title": "Nvidia’s Biggest Threat Is This: Everyone Is Desperate to Stop Paying Nvidia Prices.",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-06",
  "article_opening": "Nvidia’s Biggest Threat Is This: Everyone Is Desperate to Stop Paying Nvidia Prices.\nNVIDIA (NASDAQ:NVDA | NVDA Price Prediction) owns the AI compute market, but every major customer is spending billions to buy less of what it sells. NVIDIA carries a $4.75 trillion market cap and sits between a 52-week low of $158.18…\nBy Omor Ibne Ehsan\nNVIDIA posted 85% revenue growth to $82 billion in Q1, but half its data center sales come from hyperscalers actively funding custom AI chip alternatives.\nAmazon",
  "passage": "…of $236.26. Q1 FY27 revenue came in at $81.61 billion, up 85.2% year over year, with data center revenue of $75.25 billion.\nAbout 50% of that data center number comes from hyperscalers, the same companies bankrolling Amazon Trainium, Google TPU, Microsoft Maia and Meta MTIA.\nGrowth accelerates at NVIDIA’s scale. Management guided Q2 FY27 revenue to $91.0 billion with non-GAAP gross margin holding at 75%. Networking revenue grew 199% year over year to $14.8 billion, evidence the moat extends past GPUs into InfiniBand, Spectrum-X and NVLink.\nBlackwell Ultra is ramping, Rubin was announced, and Jensen Huang called the AI factory buildout “the largest infrastructure expansion in human history.” The dividend raised to $0.25 quarterly and an additional $80 billion buyback was authorized.\nAll of this says NVDA stock is set to keep delivering, as long as the broader market remains bullish.\nThe customer list is the threat. Amazon has disclosed Trainium is now a multi-billion-dollar business, and every hyperscaler funding NVIDIA’s data center segment also funds an alternative. Custom silicon “not only gives you a differentiation factor where you can be cheaper than competitors, but it also allows you to have some leverage over NVIDIA in negotiations.”\nChina data center compute revenue is effectively zero, and Colette Kress (Nvidia’s CFO) said losing that market, which NVIDIA sizes at “close to about $50 billion in the future,” would be material. Supply commitments of $119 billi…",
  "statement": "The dividend raised to $0.25 quarterly and an additional $80 billion buyback was authorized."
 },
 {
  "id": "G482",
  "article_id": "gnews:eeaf70fb8a11065d200d772a",
  "title": "Why Nvidia-Backed Nebius Group Stock Skyrocketed Thursday Morning",
  "publication_day": "2026-09-17",
  "article_opening": "Nebius Group ( NBIS +1.39% ) stock surged sharply higher Thursday morning, gaining as much as 11.2%. As of 2:27 p.m. ET, the stock had given back most of those gains, but was still up 2.1%.\nThe catalyst that sent the artificial intelligence (AI) and neocloud specialist higher was a report that the company is raising some prices.\nNebius is one of several neocloud operators that stockpile graphics processing units (GPUs) and provide on-demand AI processing. The company announced that it will raise",
  "passage": "Nebius Group ( NBIS +1.39% ) stock surged sharply higher Thursday morning, gaining as much as 11.2%. As of 2:27 p.m. ET, the stock had given back most of those gains, but was still up 2.1%.\nThe catalyst that sent the artificial intelligence (AI) and neocloud specialist higher was a report that the company is raising some prices.\nNebius is one of several neocloud operators that stockpile graphics processing units (GPUs) and provide on-demand AI processing. The company announced that it will raise the price for access to on-demand Nvidia (NASDAQ: NVDA) GPUs by roughly 20%, effective Oct. 1.\nH100 chips -- $4.50 per GPU hour, up 17%\nH200 chips -- $5.40 per GPU hour, up 20%\nB200 chips -- $8.50 per GPU hour, up 19%\nB300 chips -- $9.50 per GPU hour, up 21%\nDaniel Newman, CEO of global technology advisory firm The Futurum Group, addressed the increase in a post on X, saying, \"AI demand remains off the charts. [Nebius] raising prices 20% is just another example of that demand.\"\nNebius is recognized by Nvidia as a Reference Platform Cloud Partner, a designation reserved for select cloud providers that operate large-scale AI clusters optimized with Nvidia's reference architecture. Moreover, the chipmaker holds a 9.3% stake in Nebius, which includes a $2 billion investment announced ear…",
  "statement": "The company announced that it will raise the price for access to on-demand Nvidia (NASDAQ: NVDA) GPUs by roughly 20%, effective Oct. 1."
 },
